      "reason": "The structural clipping is confirmed. Numbered unit 10 begins on printed 91/PDF129 and continues through the first four text lines of printed 92/PDF130, ending with the blessing immediately before XLIX. Falling, not rising, the hand/raising clause, denial of the Lord of Spirits and his Anointed, and the blessing all belong to printed XLVIII.10. This establishes the unit boundary, not approval of every candidate glyph or witness selection. The candidate's doubled opening asterisk sequence and exact verbal endings still require confirmation.",
      "suggested_text": "On the day of their distress there will be rest on earth. They will fall before them and will not rise, and no one will take them with his hand and lift them up, because they denied the Lord of Spirits and his Anointed. May the name of the Lord of Spirits be blessed.",
      "footnote_changes": "Treat the suggested English as provisional, not approved. Replace a's obsolete 'short clause alone' explanation and attach b to 'rest.' Explain that the condemned rulers remain the subject of falling and denial; 'before them' most naturally points back to the righteous/chosen, but printed note 1 records singular/plural variation in that expression. Check the fall-verb reading against note 2, which also records q's omission. Note 5 distinguishes the selected hand wording from a plural-hands variant. Crucially, note 7 says g omits the Anointed expression and the four following words through the end of the blessing. Note 8 is anchored at the conjunction before the blessing and must not automatically be expanded into omission of the whole blessing by t and u. Notes 6 and 9 concern forms of the Spirits title, not authority to delete the clauses. Retain the Anointed and blessing when translating the selected printed text, while disclosing the omission evidence. Correct this unit's page span to PDF129–130; the whole chapter spans PDF128–130. Do not move this continuation into chapter 49 because of an existing file split, or turn the rulers' judgment into universal judgment.",
